// Gen9 halve (op 10): the scene under a glass window made its small picture, as the processor's memory_halve_u32 three times makes it
// (src/canvas/compositor/glass.c's compositor_frost, src/lib.c): each small pixel the mean of its block of 8 by 8, a level at a time, the two
// rows of a pair rounded up ((a + b + 1) >> 1 a byte) and then the two columns rounded down ((a + b) >> 1), so the picture does not grow
// lighter. A thread is eight small pixels (the card's time is the threads' more than their instructions'): walker group X (r0.1) their
// column of eight and Y (r0.6) their row. It reads its eight blocks one after another (binding table entry 1: 32 bytes by 8 rows at byte
// 256 X + 32 k of row 8 Y) and writes the eight pixels (entry 0: 32 bytes at byte 32 X of row Y), every one of them whatever the row's
// width: the pitches hold a row's last thread whole. A row of a block is a register, its B and R bytes in the words of one register and
// its G and A bytes in another, so a byte mean is a word's (avg, add and shr on words: no byte is a source, which Gen11 and later refuse).
// The pixels made are kept in r5, moved a place down as each comes. Written by the scratch generator genglass.py. Assemble with:
// iga64 -a -p=9 src/canvas/gpu_i915_halve.iga (the words are in src/canvas/gpu_i915.asm).
